import{_ as s,o as t,c as a,ah as i}from"./chunks/framework.ojwJKwrc.js";const k=JSON.parse('{"title":"Execution Semantics","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/execution-semantics.md","filePath":"guide/execution-semantics.md"}'),o={name:"guide/execution-semantics.md"};function n(c,e,d,l,r,h){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="execution-semantics" tabindex="-1">Execution Semantics <a class="header-anchor" href="#execution-semantics" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Execution Semantics&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The clock-driving operations of Celox are defined by IEEE 1800 scheduling. Each host call of the event-based <code>Simulator</code> is one time slot in which a fixed SystemVerilog statement executes, and each clock or reset component of a Veryl native testbench is a fixed piece of SystemVerilog. An IEEE 1800 simulator running that code observes the same values as Celox does.</p><p>In the code below, <code>s</code> is the signal of an event (a clock or reset port), <code>A</code> its active level and <code>I</code> its inactive one. <code>A</code> is 1 for a positive-edge clock and an active-high reset, and 0 for a negative-edge clock and an active-low reset. For Veryl&#39;s abstract <code>clock</code> and <code>reset</code> types it follows the build&#39;s clock and reset types.</p><p>Reset polarity and synchronicity belong to the design, not to these rules. Veryl emits <code>always_ff (clk, rst) { if_reset ... }</code> as <code>always_ff @(posedge clk, negedge rst_n) if (!rst_n) ...</code> for an asynchronous active-low reset and as <code>always_ff @(posedge clk) if (!rst_n) ...</code> for a synchronous one, and the IEEE 1800 scheduler produces each behavior from the signal moves alone.</p><h2 id="the-simulator-api" tabindex="-1">The <code>Simulator</code> API <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-simulator-api" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The \`Simulator\` API&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A <code>Simulator</code> has no simulation time, but it schedules as an IEEE 1800 simulation does. Each host call is one time slot (IEEE 1800-2023 4.4): the call&#39;s statement executes in that slot, the slot runs until every region is empty, and the next call starts a later slot. A read (<code>get</code>, <code>sim.dut.x</code>) between two calls sees the completed slot.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Statement of the slot</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>set(x, v)</code>, <code>sim.dut.x = v</code></td><td><code>x = v;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>edge(e, Rising)</code>, <code>posedge(e)</code></td><td><code>s &lt;= 1;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>edge(e, Falling)</code>, <code>negedge(e)</code></td><td><code>s &lt;= 0;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>active_edge(e)</code> / <code>inactive_edge(e)</code></td><td><code>s &lt;= A;</code> / <code>s &lt;= I;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>edges([(e1, l1), …, (ek, lk)])</code></td><td><code>s1 &lt;= l1; … sk &lt;= lk;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>tick(e)</code></td><td><code>s &lt;= A;</code> then, in the next slot, <code>s &lt;= I;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>tick(e, n)</code></td><td><code>tick(e)</code> <code>n</code> times</td></tr><tr><td><code>tick_events([e1, …, ek])</code></td><td><code>s1 &lt;= A1; … sk &lt;= Ak;</code> then, in the next slot, <code>s1 &lt;= I1; … sk &lt;= Ik;</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The host is not a process of the design, so no delay appears above: a slot is the unit in which the design responds to one statement. To run the same sequence on a SystemVerilog simulator, write the statements into one <code>initial</code> process and follow each with a delay of one time precision unit. A <code>Simulator</code> rejects designs with delays, so the length of that unit is not observable.</p><p>Some consequences:</p><ul><li>A tick is one clock period. Every domain sensitive to either edge runs: the event&#39;s own domain on the active edge, domains of the opposite edge (<code>@(negedge clk)</code>, a register clocked by <code>~clk</code>), and clocks computed from the signal, such as <code>clk &amp; en</code>, on whichever edge they see. The signal ends at its inactive level.</li><li>A signal already at its active level takes no active edge, as <code>s &lt;= A</code> changes nothing. A tick of a two-state active-low reset that starts at 0 only releases it; assert a reset with <code>negedge(rst)</code> or <code>active_edge(rst)</code>.</li><li>Domains triggered by one slot sample the state from before it and update together, as nonblocking assignments do. Coincident edges of several clocks (<code>edges</code>, <code>tick_events</code>) sample each other&#39;s values from before their shared slot. A clock the slot itself moves, such as a register-driven divider, rises inside the slot after that commit, and its domain runs in the same slot.</li><li>In a four-state design an unknown level counts as either level: <code>x</code> to 1 is a rising edge and <code>x</code> to 0 a falling one (IEEE 1800-2023 Table 9-2).</li></ul><p><code>set</code> and writes through <code>sim.dut</code> must not move an event signal or a clock computed from one: Celox runs domains only for the edge calls above. Drive those signals with <code>edge</code>, <code>tick</code> and their variants.</p><h3 id="resets-from-the-host" tabindex="-1">Resets from the host <a class="header-anchor" href="#resets-from-the-host" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Resets from the host&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A reset held for <code>n</code> clock periods is the active edge of the reset, <code>n</code> ticks of the clock, and the inactive edge of the reset:</p><div class="language-typescript vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">typescript</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">sim.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">activeEdge</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(rst);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">sim.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">tick</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(clk, n);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">sim.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">inactiveEdge</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(rst);</span></span></code></pre></div><p>An asynchronous reset domain runs in the first slot, before any clock edge; a synchronous one resets at the first tick. The sequence is the same slots as the testbench&#39;s <code>rst.assert(n)</code> below.</p><h3 id="several-clocks" tabindex="-1">Several clocks <a class="header-anchor" href="#several-clocks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Several clocks&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A tick is one period of its event, so it cannot express a phase relation between two clock inputs. For clocks with a frequency ratio, such as <code>clk</code> and a <code>clk_half</code> of twice its period, drive each instant with <code>edges</code>:</p><div class="language-typescript vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">typescript</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">sim.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">edges</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">([[clk, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;posedge&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">], [clkHalf, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;posedge&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">]]);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">sim.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">edges</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">([[clk, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;negedge&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">]]);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">sim.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">edges</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">([[clk, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;posedge&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">], [clkHalf, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;negedge&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">]]);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">sim.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">edges</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">([[clk, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;negedge&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">]]);</span></span></code></pre></div><p>or use the timed <a href="./writing-tests.html#time-based-simulation"><code>Simulation</code></a> with one <code>addClock</code> per input. Alternating <code>tick_events([clk, clk_half])</code> with <code>tick(clk)</code> gives the same results only to domains that see the rising edges: it makes <code>clk_half</code> fall with <code>clk</code>&#39;s falling edge instead of its next rising one.</p><h2 id="veryl-native-testbenches" tabindex="-1">Veryl native testbenches <a class="header-anchor" href="#veryl-native-testbenches" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Veryl native testbenches&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The initial blocks of a Veryl testbench are SystemVerilog <code>initial</code> processes, and <code>$finish()</code> is <code>$finish</code>. Unlike the host, these processes have simulation time: the components below are SystemVerilog code, and their delays are the testbench&#39;s time.</p><h3 id="tb-clock-gen" tabindex="-1"><code>$tb::clock_gen</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#tb-clock-gen" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;\`$tb::clock_gen\`&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>inst clk: $tb::clock_gen #(period: P);</code> is a clock generator that runs from time 0, whether or not a process waits on it. <code>P</code> defaults to 2 and is at least 2; with <code>H = ceil(P / 2)</code> and <code>L = floor(P / 2)</code>:</p><div class="language-systemverilog vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">systemverilog</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>bit clk = 1&#39;b0;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>initial forever begin</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  clk &lt;= 1&#39;b1; #H;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  clk &lt;= 1&#39;b0; #L;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>end</span></span></code></pre></div><h3 id="clk-next-n" tabindex="-1"><code>clk.next(n)</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#clk-next-n" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;\`clk.next(n)\`&quot;">​</a></h3><div class="language-systemverilog vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">systemverilog</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>task automatic clk_next(int unsigned n);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  if (n != 0) begin</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    repeat (n) @(posedge clk);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    #P;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  end</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>endtask</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>clk.next()</code> is <code>clk.next(1)</code>.</p><ul><li>The process resumes a period after the <code>n</code>-th rising edge it waits for, just before the next one. It then observes the state after both edges of that period: the clock is low, and negative-edge domains have run.</li><li>The clock rises through a nonblocking assignment, after the processes that resume at the same time have run. What a process writes between two <code>next</code> calls is therefore sampled by the rising edge that follows, without a race.</li><li>Several initial processes, and several clocks of different periods, need no further rule: they are concurrent SystemVerilog processes. A clock nobody waits on keeps running and clocking its domains.</li></ul><h3 id="tb-reset-gen" tabindex="-1"><code>$tb::reset_gen</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#tb-reset-gen" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;\`$tb::reset_gen\`&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>inst rst: $tb::reset_gen #(cycles: C) (clk);</code> is a reset net of the testbench&#39;s <code>reset</code> type, deasserted from time 0:</p><div class="language-systemverilog vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">systemverilog</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>bit rst = I;</span></span></code></pre></div><h3 id="rst-assert-n" tabindex="-1"><code>rst.assert(n)</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#rst-assert-n" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;\`rst.assert(n)\`&quot;">​</a></h3><p>With <code>P</code> the period of the generator&#39;s clock:</p><div class="language-systemverilog vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">systemverilog</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>task automatic rst_assert(int unsigned n);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  if (n == 0) n = 1;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  rst = A;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  repeat (n) @(posedge clk);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  #P;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  rst = I;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>endtask</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>rst.assert()</code> is <code>rst.assert(C)</code>, and <code>C</code> defaults to 3.</p><ul><li>The assertion is a blocking write at the point the process has reached, like any other testbench write. An asynchronous reset domain runs at that instant, with no clock edge, whether or not its clock is running. A synchronous reset domain resets at the next rising edge.</li><li>The level is held for <code>n</code> periods of <code>clk</code>: the <code>n</code> rising edges starting with the next one, or with the one at the same instant, since the clock rises after the process has written, and the period after the last of them. Both kinds of reset see <code>n</code> reset edges and are released before edge <code>n + 1</code>.</li><li>The process resumes, and the reset releases, at the phase at which <code>next</code> resumes: a period after the last awaited edge, just before the next one. Writes that follow are sampled by that edge without a race, and <code>rst.assert(n); clk.next(m);</code> is <code>n + m</code> periods.</li><li><code>rst.assert(0)</code> is <code>rst.assert(1)</code>. A reset released in the slot that asserted it is a glitch in SystemVerilog and is not given a meaning.</li></ul><p>This page defines <code>$tb::clock_gen</code>, <code>clk.next</code>, <code>$tb::reset_gen</code> and <code>rst.assert</code>. The other testbench components, <code>$tb::file</code> and <code>$tb::random</code>, take no part in scheduling.</p>`,37)])])}const g=s(o,[["render",n]]);export{k as __pageData,g as default};
